just a reminder now of what's in the law  and why it's caused such widespread  opposition so it creates a pathway to  citizenship to minorities who face  religious persecution in Pakistan  Bangladesh and Afghanistan but it  excludes Muslims opposition parties on  protesters say it violates India's  secular Constitution but that's not the  only dynamic playing out in states  bordering Bangladesh West Bengal is  controlled by an opposition party the  ruling BJP is being accused of seeking  electoral gains for the regional vote  due in 2021 by opening the door to more  Hindu refugees and migrants while in  nearby northeastern states especially  Assam indigenous people fear an influx  of hindu bengalis they say that could  put their language and culture under  threat let speak to Tarun chitin he's a  professor of law and legal theory at the  University of Oxford he's also the  general editor of Indian Law Review he's  joining us from London thanks very much  for being with us on the al Jazeera news  hour so interestingly our reporter from  New Delhi was just saying that the  students who are protesting right now in  front of the universities are discussing  the importance of upholding the Indian  Constitution yet you say that the  government is using unconstitutional  means to curb freedom of expression what  do you mean by that so look this  government has been chipping away at  India's constitutional fundamentals for  the last six years but this act does it  so blatantly and so expressly that  there's no plausible deniability Indian  institutions constitutional institutions  have failed just to stop the government  from doing what it has done to the  Constitution and ultimately it seems of  the people have decided that it's upon  them to save India's pluralistic secular  democratic constitution so it's it's  really amazing to see how the  Constitution's preamble has become the  symbol of these protests you know these  are not being organized by a political  party there is no social movement or NGO  behind them  and you know the preamble being read out  publicly collectively in almost every  protest that has been happening around  this act is is really astonishing of  course the state's response has been  remarkably disproportionate and and  blatantly unconstitutional so clamp  downs on freedom of speech Internet is  being suspended you know in turn is  being suspended in Kashmir for the last  four months but but in places where  protests happen you know it's hard to  organize these protests without social  media so governments have been shutting  down mobile internet connections  telephone lines advisories to  universities asking them to monitor  students social media posts the police  has been imposing what's called section  144 of the Criminal Procedure Code which  basically makes it illegal for any  crowds to assemble which again without  although it's permitted in law it has to  be for very good reason for protecting  law and order situation that's what I  wanted to ask you about specifically in  fact section 144 because in the  Constitution it does authorize officials  to prohibit among other things a  gathering as you're saying of more than  four people if they fear a possible  violation of law and order so so you're  saying that the the government is not  acting within the Constitution when it  implements that section 144 so section  144 is not a constitutional provision it  is a provision in the Criminal Procedure  Code which which is a legacy of the  colonial administration to India what  the Constitution protects is the freedom  of assembly and the freedom of the  citizens assembly can only be restricted  on certain reasonable grounds and only  proportionately so yes the government  has the power under form 144 to clamp  down on assembly but it can do so only  for very good reasons and it has to  follow proper procedures and in a  substantive reason so so it cannot for  example what the government cannot do is  use 144 to stop the expression of  citizens dissent or a citizen any  citizens group that wants to do  advocate for a change in policy or show  it's disaffection for any government  governmental policy that is not  permissible by the Indian Constitution  and that is what the government is doing  why is the citizenship law that's caused  all these protests seen by many people  as a threat to Indian democracy as a  whole so there been a lot of threats  during a democracy of late but this is  the most blatant because for the first  time in India's democratic independent  history we are going to write religion  as a qualification into our citizenship  laws the law in giving fast-track  citizenship to basically non-muslims but  also some other residues ATS Muslims  some of these communities are not  covered but Hindus Sikhs and Jews are  covered that is that is just so  blatantly against the very core of the  Indian Constitution identity which is to  seek to to build a nation that is not  based on religious identity that is  secular that is pluralistic that is  diverse and it's celebrated that  diversity for the last 70 odd years so  that it's it's the explicitness you know  a lot of these government's policies  have been sort of dog-whistle politics  of everybody knows the code is  anti-muslim but now the government felt  emboldened to do not feel the need for  force for dog whistles it's just do you  think it's a government has been caught  off guard by the size of these protests  and the ongoing protests and and how do  you see this playing playing out going  forward is there any way that the  government will go back on this decision  in this law it's not just a government  that has been caught off guard  it's the protests of Survivor have  surprised everyone including people like  me you know academics have been watching  Indian politics and constitution for a  long time nobody expected this because  things were relatively quiet over the  Ayodhya judgement over the lynchings  that have been happening in India for  the last few years so so why why now and  why so suddenly why on this issue is  it's a little bit surprising and I think  people are thinking about what what  triggered it I think that is  proportionate use of violence against do  in jamia including students who were not  in protesting but studying in libraries  police beating them up the videos that  circulated I think they they were the  catalysts and there's also a building up  of of disgruntlement against the  government that has just poured out on  the streets not just by Muslims you know  people from all communities have have  joined this protest the government has  been caught off guard I don't know  whether they have the reflexively you  know the flexibility and the  responsiveness to to react to the  protests but what is needed is they have  to repeal this law I think I think  Parliament needs to be recalled  immediately the amendments have to be  repealed but a lot older laura's has to  be done because what this government's  intentions in relation to its Muslim  population to India's Muslim population  is is deeply under suspicion and the  country has to prove its good faith now  by passing an anti-discrimination law  and a host of other things  Khaitan we thank you very much for  joining us from London
 

